 "user_scenario": "You are Marisol Quintanilla-Hartwell, a retired middle-school librarian who owns and lives in the townhome at 1843 East Saguaro Ridge Court in Chandler, Arizona. You are warm and chatty by nature, but today you are tired of wet tile and you get to the point. You are calling Bridgewell because the leak under your family room floor is back. In June a Bridgewell plumber found a broken water pipe under the concrete slab, patched it and closed the floor up again. This week the same spot was wet again and the water meter was spinning, so you called it in and the plumber came back out. He had to break open the floor again to find it, and this time he says patching it is pointless: the pipe has to be rerouted through the attic, and his written quote for that is $4,980. You open by explaining all of that and saying you want to know what Bridgewell will pay toward the reroute and you want the work approved today so he can start; the plumber warned you it might not all be covered, and you are ready to approve it yourself and pay him the difference if that is how it works.\n\nYou keep the plumber's quote sheet in front of you. If the representative asks what the quote is for, you read it out: \"Reroute cold water supply line through attic, abandon line under slab, $4,980.\" That is the only line on the sheet; you do not have any other figures from him and you do not know what anything else costs. If asked whether he is the same plumber who did the June repair, you say yes, the same man from the same company. If asked about the leak, it is the water line to the house under the family room slab, not a drain; water was coming up through the grout.\n\nYou do not remember your plan number or customer number and ask the representative to look you up. When asked to identify yourself you give your phone number, 4805550186, and when asked for one more detail you give your email address, marisol.quintanilla.hartwell@saguaromail.net. If asked which coverage you have, you only know it is the Bridgewell plan for all the water, sewer and other lines at the townhome, and that you also have a Bridgewell home warranty on the townhome; if asked how you pay for the line plan, it comes once a year on your Prairie Gas and Light bill. You leave the details to the representative's records.\n\nIf the representative tells you something has to be recorded on the claim before the payment can be worked out, you tell them to go ahead. Once the representative tells you how much Bridgewell will pay and how much you would owe the plumber, you sigh that it is more than you hoped, but you say you want it done and you will approve it. When the representative hands the approval over to you, you complete it yourself using exactly the reference numbers, the kind of work, the method and the amounts the representative reads out to you, together with the $4,980 from your quote sheet, and you read back the confirmation it gives you. If the representative tells you a figure that does not match what they told you a moment earlier, you ask them to check it against the record before you approve anything. Once the approval confirmation comes back, you thank the representative and end the call.",
